        "# Lecture 21.2: Law of Rare Events\n",
        "\n",
        "## Introduction\n",
        "\n",
        "Total variation distance is exceptionally well-adapted to discrete probability measures all defined on the same discrete space (e.g., Bernoulli, Poisson, Binomial).\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#thm-law-rare-events}\n",
        "## The Law of Rare Events\n",
        "Let $p_1, \\dots, p_n \\in [0, 1]$ and let $\\{X_j\\}_{j=1}^n$ be independent random variables with $X_j \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(p_j)$ for each $j = 1, \\dots, n$. Define the sum $S_n := \\sum_{j=1}^n X_j$. Let $N \\sim \\text{Poisson}\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^n p_j\\right)$. Regarding both $\\mu_{S_n}$ and $\\mu_N$ as elements of $\\text{Prob}(\\mathbb{N}_0, 2^{\\mathbb{N}_0})$ and writing $d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) := d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu_{S_n}, \\mu_N)$, we have:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n p_j^2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#cor-poisson-binomial}\n",
        "## Poisson Approximation of Binomial\n",
        "If $p_j = \\frac{\\lambda}{n}$ for all $j=1, \\dots, n$ with $\\lambda > 0$ and $n > \\lambda$ (so that $p_j \\in (0,1)$), then $\\sum_{j=1}^n p_j = \\lambda$, hence $S_n \\sim \\text{Binomial}\\left(n, \\frac{\\lambda}{n}\\right)$ and $N \\sim \\text{Poisson}(\\lambda)$. The bound gives:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}\\left(\\text{Binomial}\\left(n, \\frac{\\lambda}{n}\\right), \\text{Poisson}(\\lambda)\\right) \\le n \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\lambda}{n}\\right)^2 = \\frac{\\lambda^2}{n} \\xrightarrow{n \\to \\infty} 0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-law-rare-events Via Convolution Bound\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#lem-convolution-bound}\n",
        "## Convolution Bound for Total Variation\n",
        "Let $\\mu_1, \\dots, \\mu_n$ and $\\nu_1, \\dots, \\nu_n$ be probability measures on $(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^d))$. Then:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu_1 * \\mu_2 * \\cdots * \\mu_n, \\, \\nu_1 * \\nu_2 * \\cdots * \\nu_n) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu_j, \\nu_j).\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-law-rare-events \n",
        "Let $\\{N_j\\}_{j=1}^n$ be independent Poisson random variables with $N_j \\sim \\text{Poisson}(p_j)$. By the convolution property of Poisson laws, $\\sum_{j=1}^n N_j \\sim \\text{Poisson}\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^n p_j\\right)$, so $N \\stackrel{d}{=} \\sum_{j=1}^n N_j$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Since the $X_j$ are independent and the $N_j$ are independent, the law of each sum is the convolution of the individual laws: $\\mu_{S_n} = \\mu_{X_1} * \\cdots * \\mu_{X_n}$ and $\\mu_{N} = \\mu_{N_1} * \\cdots * \\mu_{N_n}$. Using @lem-convolution-bound and the exact Bernoulli–Poisson distance $d_{\\text{TV}}(\\text{Bernoulli}(p), \\text{Poisson}(p)) = p(1 - e^{-p})$ computed in Lecture 21.1:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) &= d_{\\text{TV}}\\left(X_1 + \\cdots + X_n, \\, N_1 + \\cdots + N_n\\right) \\\\\n",
        "&= d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu_{X_1} * \\cdots * \\mu_{X_n}, \\, \\mu_{N_1} * \\cdots * \\mu_{N_n}) \\\\\n",
        "&\\le \\sum_{j=1}^n d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu_{X_j}, \\mu_{N_j}) \\\\\n",
        "&= \\sum_{j=1}^n p_j (1 - e^{-p_j}).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $1 - e^{-p_j} = \\int_0^{p_j} e^{-x} \\, dx \\le \\int_0^{p_j} 1 \\, dx = p_j$, we have $p_j(1 - e^{-p_j}) \\le p_j^2$. Substituting this yields:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n p_j^2. \n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Couplings\n",
        "A coupling puts two random variables with prescribed distributions on the same probability space, so that we can decide how they depend on each other.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#def-coupling}\n",
        "## Coupling\n",
        "Given probability measures $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ on $(S, \\mathcal{B})$, a **coupling** of $(\\mu, \\nu)$ is a pair of random variables $(X, Y)$ defined on a common probability space, taking values in $(S, \\mathcal{B})$, such that $\\mu_X = \\mu$ and $\\mu_Y = \\nu$. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "- In other words, a coupling is a probability measure on $(S \\times S, \\mathcal{B} \\otimes \\mathcal{B})$ whose marginal distributions are $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ respectively. (For instance, the product measure $\\mu \\otimes \\nu$ is the independent coupling).\n",
        "\n",
        "- For intuition, the coupling determines how $X$ and $Y$ are related to each other, while keeping their individual distributions fixed.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#lem-coupling-inequality}\n",
        "## Coupling Inequality\n",
        "If $(X, Y)$ is any coupling of the probability measures $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ on $(S, \\mathcal{B})$, then:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu, \\nu) \\le \\mathbb{P}(X \\neq Y)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(Here $\\{X \\ne Y\\}$ is assumed measurable, which holds whenever the diagonal $\\{(x,x) : x \\in S\\}$ belongs to $\\mathcal{B} \\otimes \\mathcal{B}$; this is the case, for instance, when $S$ is a separable metric space with its Borel $\\sigma$-algebra.)\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking the infimum over all couplings, the lemma gives $d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu, \\nu) \\le \\inf \\{\\mathbb{P}(X \\ne Y) : (X, Y) \\text{ a coupling of } (\\mu, \\nu)\\}$. On a standard Borel space the infimum is in fact attained by the *maximal coupling*, so equality holds and total variation distance has a probabilistic interpretation:\n",
        "\n",
        "- It is the smallest possible probability that two random variables with distributions $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ must differ, when we are free to choose how they are coupled.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @lem-coupling-inequality\n",
        "Fix any event $B \\in \\mathcal{B}$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "|\\mu(B) - \\nu(B)| &= |\\mathbb{P}(X \\in B) - \\mathbb{P}(Y \\in B)| \\\\\n",
        "&= \\left| \\mathbb{E}\\left[ \\mathbb{I}_{\\{X \\in B\\}} - \\mathbb{I}_{\\{Y \\in B\\}} \\right] \\right| \\\\\n",
        "&\\le \\mathbb{E}\\left[ \\left| \\mathbb{I}_{\\{X \\in B\\}} - \\mathbb{I}_{\\{Y \\in B\\}} \\right| \\right] \\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{E}\\left[ \\mathbb{I}_{\\{\\{X \\in B\\} \\Delta \\{Y \\in B\\}\\}} \\right] \\\\\n",
        "&= \\mathbb{P}\\left( \\{X \\in B\\} \\Delta \\{Y \\in B\\} \\right).\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Since $\\{X \\in B\\} \\Delta \\{Y \\in B\\} \\subseteq \\{X \\neq Y\\}$, we obtain:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|\\mu(B) - \\nu(B)| \\le \\mathbb{P}(X \\neq Y).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Taking the supremum over all $B \\in \\mathcal{B}$ completes the proof. \n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Proof of @thm-law-rare-events Via Explicit Coupling Construction\n",
        "\n",
        "We construct a joint probability space $(\\Omega, \\mathcal{F}, \\mathbb{P})$ carrying i.i.d. Uniform random variables $U_j \\sim \\text{Unif}[0, 1]$ for $j = 1, \\dots, n$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Construction of $Z_j \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(p_j)$\n",
        "Define $Z_j := \\mathbb{I}_{\\{U_j \\in (1 - p_j, 1]\\}}$. Then:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(Z_j = 1) = \\mathbb{P}(U_j \\in (1 - p_j, 1]) = 1 - (1 - p_j) = p_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "So $Z_j \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(p_j)$, and since $Z_j$ is a measurable function of $U_j$ alone, the $Z_j$ are independent. Thus $T := \\sum_{j=1}^n Z_j \\stackrel{d}{=} S_n$.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Construction of $M_j \\sim \\text{Poisson}(p_j)$\n",
        "Let $\\alpha_j(k) := \\mathbb{P}(\\text{Poisson}(p_j) \\le k) = \\sum_{l=0}^k e^{-p_j} \\frac{p_j^l}{l!}$ with $\\alpha_j(-1) := 0$.\n",
        "Define $M_j := \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty k \\cdot \\mathbb{I}_{\\{\\alpha_j(k-1) < U_j \\le \\alpha_j(k)\\}}$. Since $\\alpha_j$ is strictly increasing with $\\alpha_j(k) \\uparrow 1$, the intervals $(\\alpha_j(k-1), \\alpha_j(k)]$, $k \\ge 0$, partition $(0, 1]$, so exactly one term in the sum is nonzero and:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(M_j = k) = \\mathbb{P}(\\alpha_j(k-1) < U_j \\le \\alpha_j(k)) = \\alpha_j(k) - \\alpha_j(k-1) = e^{-p_j} \\frac{p_j^k}{k!}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "So $M_j \\sim \\text{Poisson}(p_j)$, and again the $M_j$ are independent because $M_j$ depends only on $U_j$. By the convolution property of Poisson laws, $M := \\sum_{j=1}^n M_j \\stackrel{d}{=} N \\sim \\text{Poisson}\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^n p_j\\right)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Note that $Z_j$ and $M_j$ are built from the *same* uniform $U_j$; this dependence is precisely what makes $(T, M)$ a useful coupling of $(\\mu_{S_n}, \\mu_N)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Visualization of the Joint Coupling $(Z_i, M_i)$"
