        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "### Evaluating the Difference $\\mathbb{P}(Z_j \\neq M_j)$\n",
        "\n",
        "Since $T \\stackrel{d}{=} S_n$ and $M \\stackrel{d}{=} N$, the pair $(T, M)$ is a coupling of $(\\mu_{S_n}, \\mu_N)$, so @lem-coupling-inequality applies. Moreover $\\{T \\ne M\\} \\subseteq \\bigcup_{j=1}^n \\{Z_j \\ne M_j\\}$, because $Z_j = M_j$ for every $j$ forces $T = M$; the union bound then gives:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) \\le \\mathbb{P}(T \\neq M) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n \\mathbb{P}(Z_j \\neq M_j)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "To compute $\\mathbb{P}(Z_j \\ne M_j)$, note the elementary inequality $1 - p_j \\le e^{-p_j} = \\alpha_j(0) \\le \\alpha_j(1)$, so the interval $[0,1]$ splits into four consecutive pieces:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Range of $U_j$ | $Z_j$ | $M_j$ | Agree? |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| $[0,\\, 1 - p_j]$ | $0$ | $0$ | yes |\n",
        "| $(1 - p_j,\\, \\alpha_j(0)]$ | $1$ | $0$ | no |\n",
        "| $(\\alpha_j(0),\\, \\alpha_j(1)]$ | $1$ | $1$ | yes |\n",
        "| $(\\alpha_j(1),\\, 1]$ | $1$ | $\\ge 2$ | no |\n",
        "\n",
        "Adding the lengths of the two disagreement intervals:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "\\mathbb{P}(Z_j \\neq M_j) &= [\\alpha_j(0) - (1 - p_j)] + [1 - \\alpha_j(1)] \\\\\n",
        "&= [e^{-p_j} - (1 - p_j)] + [1 - (e^{-p_j} + e^{-p_j} p_j)] \\\\\n",
        "&= p_j - p_j e^{-p_j} = p_j (1 - e^{-p_j}) \\le p_j^2,\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "where the last step again uses $1 - e^{-p_j} \\le p_j$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Summing over $j=1, \\dots, n$ yields:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n p_j^2. \n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Optimal Transport & Wasserstein Distance\n",
        "\n",
        "Couplings play a foundational role in large deviations, stochastic analysis, and optimal transport.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#def-Wasserstein-Distance}\n",
        "## Wasserstein Distance\n",
        "For $p \\ge 1$, let $\\text{Prob}_p(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}(\\mathbb{R}^d))$ denote the set of Borel probability measures $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ with finite $p$-th moment $\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} |x|^p \\mu(dx) < \\infty\\right)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **$p$-Wasserstein distance** between $\\mu, \\nu \\in \\text{Prob}_p(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "W_p(\\mu, \\nu) := \\left( \\inf \\left\\{ \\mathbb{E}\\left[|X - Y|^p\\right] : (X, Y) \\text{ is a coupling of } (\\mu, \\nu) \\right\\} \\right)^{1/p}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Unlike $d_{\\text{TV}}$, the metric $W_p$ incorporates the underlying geometry of $\\mathbb{R}^d$: it measures *how far apart* $X$ and $Y$ are, not merely *whether* they differ. The two topologies are not comparable on all of $\\text{Prob}_p(\\mathbb{R}^d)$:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $W_p$ convergence does not imply $d_{\\text{TV}}$ convergence: $W_p(\\delta_{1/n}, \\delta_0) = 1/n \\to 0$, while $d_{\\text{TV}}(\\delta_{1/n}, \\delta_0) = 1$ for every $n$.\n",
        "- $d_{\\text{TV}}$ convergence does not imply $W_p$ convergence: for $\\mu_n := (1 - \\tfrac1n)\\delta_0 + \\tfrac1n \\delta_{n^2}$ we have $d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu_n, \\delta_0) = \\tfrac1n \\to 0$, yet $W_p(\\mu_n, \\delta_0) = (n^{2p}/n)^{1/p} \\to \\infty$.\n",
        "\n",
        "On a set of measures all supported in a fixed bounded set of diameter $D$, however, the coupling inequality does give $W_p(\\mu, \\nu) \\le D \\cdot d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu, \\nu)^{1/p}$, so there the $W_p$-topology is genuinely **weaker** than $d_{\\text{TV}}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Takeaways\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **The Law of Rare Events is a non-asymptotic theorem, not a limit theorem.**  \n",
        "   @thm-law-rare-events states that for *any* $n$ and *any* $p_1, \\dots, p_n \\in [0,1]$, the sum of independent Bernoulli variables satisfies $d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n p_j^2$ where $N \\sim \\text{Poisson}(\\sum_j p_j)$. There is no hypothesis that the $p_j$ be small, equal, or that $n \\to \\infty$: the bound is an explicit error estimate valid at every fixed $n$, which is exactly what makes it usable in applications. The classical statement \"Binomial $\\to$ Poisson\" is the corollary @cor-poisson-binomial obtained by specializing to $p_j = \\lambda/n$, giving the quantitative rate $\\lambda^2/n$.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. **The bound is driven by the exact Bernoulli–Poisson distance $p(1 - e^{-p})$.**  \n",
        "   Both proofs reduce to the single-coordinate computation $d_{\\text{TV}}(\\text{Bernoulli}(p), \\text{Poisson}(p)) = p(1 - e^{-p})$ from Lecture 21.1, followed by the elementary bound $1 - e^{-p} \\le p$. The quadratic term $p_j^2$ is therefore a *relaxation*: the sharper conclusion $d_{\\text{TV}}(S_n, N) \\le \\sum_{j=1}^n p_j(1 - e^{-p_j})$ holds and is strictly better whenever the $p_j$ are not negligible.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. **Two proof strategies, one estimate: convolution versus coupling.**  \n",
        "   The first proof is *analytic*: @lem-convolution-bound says total variation is subadditive under convolution, so the error accumulates one coordinate at a time. The second is *probabilistic*: it builds all $2n$ variables from $n$ i.i.d. uniforms via inverse-CDF sampling and bounds the disagreement probability directly. Both routes produce the same sum $\\sum_j p_j(1 - e^{-p_j})$, since both localize the discrepancy to individual coordinates — the convolution bound is, in effect, the coupling argument done at the level of measures.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. **Coupling converts a distance between measures into a probability of disagreement.**  \n",
        "   @def-coupling fixes the marginals $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ while leaving the dependence structure free, and @lem-coupling-inequality gives $d_{\\text{TV}}(\\mu, \\nu) \\le \\mathbb{P}(X \\ne Y)$ for *every* such choice. The strategy is therefore to *design* a coupling that makes $X$ and $Y$ agree as often as possible: any coupling yields a valid upper bound, and a clever one yields a sharp bound. Here the design step is to drive $Z_j$ and $M_j$ from the same uniform $U_j$, which forces them to agree except on the two small intervals $(1 - p_j, e^{-p_j}]$ and $(\\alpha_j(1), 1]$.\n",
        "\n",
        "5. **The union bound is what makes the error additive.**  \n",
        "   The passage from the single-coordinate estimate to the global one rests on $\\{T \\ne M\\} \\subseteq \\bigcup_{j=1}^n \\{Z_j \\ne M_j\\}$: the two sums can only differ if some coordinate pair differs. This is why the total error is a *sum* of $n$ per-coordinate errors, and why the bound degrades linearly in $n$ while each term is quadratic in $p_j$ — a trade-off that is favorable precisely in the \"rare events\" regime where $\\max_j p_j$ is small relative to $1/\\sqrt{n}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "6. **Total variation and Wasserstein measure different things.**  \n",
        "   $d_{\\text{TV}}$ asks *whether* $X$ and $Y$ differ; $W_p$ of @def-Wasserstein-Distance asks *how far apart* they are, and so is sensitive to the geometry of the state space. Neither topology dominates the other on $\\text{Prob}_p(\\mathbb{R}^d)$: $\\delta_{1/n} \\to \\delta_0$ in $W_p$ but not in $d_{\\text{TV}}$, while $(1 - \\tfrac1n)\\delta_0 + \\tfrac1n \\delta_{n^2} \\to \\delta_0$ in $d_{\\text{TV}}$ but not in $W_p$. On uniformly bounded supports the comparison $W_p \\le D \\cdot d_{\\text{TV}}^{1/p}$ does hold. Total variation is the right tool here because Bernoulli and Poisson laws live on the common discrete space $\\mathbb{N}_0$, where \"differ\" is the natural unit of error."
